{%- comment -%}
  Translate a JSON-Schema `pattern` (ECMA-262 regex, which is what JSON Schema and MCP
  clients use) into a regex that the platform's `matches` filter (Ruby) evaluates the
  same way.

  Why: in ECMA-262 without the `m` flag, `^` and `$` match only at the start and end of
  the whole string. In Ruby they match at every line boundary, so `^[a-z]+$` accepts
  "abc\n123", which every validating client rejects. Outside a character class, an
  unescaped `^` becomes `\A` and an unescaped `$` becomes `\z`. Escapes (`\^`, `\$`,
  `\\`) and character classes (`[^a-z$]`) are copied unchanged.

  Other differences between the two dialects are not translated. The pattern subset
  that registry/validate_schema_node accepts keeps such patterns rare.

  @param {string} pattern - the schema's pattern
  @return {string} the equivalent pattern for `matches`
{%- endcomment -%}
{% liquid
  assign out = ''
  assign in_class = false
  assign escaped = false
  # A one-backslash string ('\' does not parse: the lexer reads \' as an escape).
  assign backslash_list = ["\\"]
  assign backslash = backslash_list.first
  assign chars = pattern | split: ''
  for ch in chars
    if escaped
      assign out = out | append: ch
      assign escaped = false
      continue
    endif
    if ch == backslash
      assign out = out | append: ch
      assign escaped = true
      continue
    endif
    if in_class
      if ch == ']'
        assign in_class = false
      endif
      assign out = out | append: ch
      continue
    endif
    if ch == '['
      assign in_class = true
      assign out = out | append: ch
    elsif ch == '^'
      assign out = out | append: '\A'
    elsif ch == '$'
      assign out = out | append: '\z'
    else
      assign out = out | append: ch
    endif
  endfor
  return out
%}
